-- Leak guard for the per-ELEMENT float box leak in the compiled
-- NativeArray.fold_float / fold_f32 runtime helpers.
--
-- native_float_arr_fold / native_f32_arr_fold materialise each stored
-- element into a FRESH march_alloc_float box before calling the closure
-- (the wire ABI passes a Float argument as an erased boxed pointer). The
-- closure treats that argument as borrowed and never releases it, so
-- without the explicit `march_decrc(elem)` after the call the box leaks:
-- one heap object per ELEMENT, unbounded in array length. An output diff
-- cannot see this -- the fold returns the right number either way -- so a
-- memory signal is the only one available, hence this probe.
-- (native_arr_fold.march remains the correctness/parity witness; this file
-- guards only memory.)
--
-- SIGNAL: live_allocs(), NOT RSS. live_allocs() reads the runtime's
-- always-on live-object gauge -- incremented in march_alloc,
-- decremented on every free-on-rc-zero path. A leak is exactly "objects
-- allocated and never freed", so this counts the defect directly: no
-- allocator free-list behaviour, no page-granularity rounding, no
-- OS/platform baseline. That last point is why it replaced peak RSS here.
-- The RSS threshold was calibrated on Darwin (healthy 48 MB, leaked 170 MB,
-- threshold 96 MB) and went RED the first time CI's ubuntu leg ran it, at
-- 128,143,360 B -- not a leak, just a ~122 MB Linux process baseline that
-- the macOS-derived band never accounted for. An absolute-RSS threshold is
-- not portable; a live-object count needs no calibration at all.
--
-- ACCUMULATOR TYPE IS LOAD-BEARING. There is a SECOND, independent leak in
-- these same helpers: when the accumulator is itself a Float, each
-- iteration's march_alloc_float result becomes the next accumulator and was
-- never released either (one more object per element on top of the element
-- box) -- inherited from the reference march_typed_array_fold. That one was
-- fixed 2026-08-20
-- (specs/progress/2026-08-20-fold-accumulator-chain-leak-fix.md) and is
-- guarded by the sibling fixture native_arr_fold_acc_leak_probe.march. An
-- INT accumulator is wire-tagged, never heap-allocated, so it contributes
-- zero live objects and this probe measures the element box in ISOLATION.
-- Do not "simplify" either fold body to `acc +. x` -- that would fold the two
-- signals together and destroy this probe's ability to name which defect
-- regressed.
--
-- Both legs are needed: the two decrc(elem) calls are separate lines in
-- separate C functions, and deleting either one alone must go RED. Because
-- the signal is a net live count and each leg's array is released when its
-- pfn returns, the two legs do not interact -- unlike peak RSS, where freed
-- pages are not handed back to the OS and the two arrays had to be summed.
--
-- Numbers, measured on this fixture at 4,000,000 elements per leg
-- (Darwin arm64, --opt 2). Both sabotage directions were actually run:
--   both decrc present        live_allocs delta =         3
--   f64 decrc(elem) deleted   live_allocs delta = 4,000,003
--   f32 decrc(elem) deleted   live_allocs delta = 4,000,003
-- Each sabotage adds exactly 4,000,000 == one leaked march_alloc_float cell
-- per element, which is the defect stated exactly rather than estimated.
-- The dune rule asserts < 1000: ~333x above the healthy figure (ample room
-- for incidental runtime objects) and 4000x below either leaked figure, so
-- it is falsifiable in both directions with an enormous margin. The healthy
-- delta is a small constant, not a per-element quantity -- if it ever grows
-- with the element count, that is a real new leak, not drift in this bound.
--
-- Init accumulator is 1 (ODD) on purpose, for the same reason as
-- native_arr_fold.march: an even init can hide a lost
-- builtin_boxed_generic_params_tbl entry behind the conditional untag.
--
-- .expected IS interpreter-produced (unlike simd_leak_probe's, which is
-- arithmetic): the tree-walker completes this fixture in ~7 minutes, slow but
-- finite, so compiled/interpreted parity here is a real observation. Do not
-- scale the element count up much further without rechecking that.
--
-- The probe prints its live_allocs delta to STDERR via IO.warn so stdout
-- stays diffable while the dune rule reads the delta out of the captured
-- stderr file -- same three-rule structure as simd_leak_probe (run / stdout
-- diff / threshold assertion). Note that live_allocs() returns a constant 0
-- in the INTERPRETER (the tree-walker allocates no march objects at all --
-- see eval.ml), which is exactly why the delta goes to stderr: the stdout
-- .expected must stay identical between the two backends.
mod NativeArrFoldLeakProbe do
  needs IO.Console

  -- Int accumulator: counts the positive elements. Reads x (so the element
  -- box really is consumed) without ever holding a Float in acc.
  pfn f64_leg() : Int do
    let arr = NativeArray.make_float(4000000, 1.0)
    NativeArray.fold_float(arr, 1, fn (acc, x) ->
      if x > 0.0 do acc + 1 else acc end)
  end

  pfn f32_leg() : Int do
    let arr = NativeArray.make_f32(4000000, 1.0)
    NativeArray.fold_f32(arr, 1, fn (acc, x) ->
      if x > 0.0 do acc + 1 else acc end)
  end

  fn main(_cap_console : Cap(IO.Console)) do
    let before = live_allocs()
    println(f64_leg())
    println(f32_leg())
    let after = live_allocs()
    IO.warn(int_to_string(after - before))
  end
end
